Source package: gnome-shell

no-screenshots-while-locked.patch
    Hides the screenshot button in quick settings while the screen is locked, and makes Print, which
    opens the screenshot UI, and Shift+Print, which saves a screenshot of the whole screen, do nothing
    there. GNOME Shell offers them on the lock screen on purpose, as its maintainer confirmed in
    https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gnome-shell/-/issues/9469: the lock screen shows nothing private,
    and screen recording, selecting a window and Alt+Print are already not offered while locked.
    Anyone at the locked machine could still save screenshots into ~/Pictures/Screenshots and replace
    what is in the clipboard with them. Upstream keeps its behavior, so this patch stays.

    The InteractiveScreenshot method of org.gnome.Shell.Screenshot, through which the screenshot
    portal lets an application ask the user for a screenshot, is left as it is and still opens the
    screenshot UI while locked.

    The keybindings are added in the constructor of ScreenshotUI in js/ui/screenshot.js, where
    restrictedModes already leaves out the lock screen for screen recording and Alt+Print, and the
    button is ScreenshotItem in js/ui/status/system.js. After a version bump, check that nothing else
    opening the screenshot UI was added with action modes which include the lock screen.

    The patch adds no test. The build runs gnome-shell's tests, which start the whole shell too, but
    none of them locks the screen or opens the screenshot UI.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login, with the screen locked:

      quick settings     should show no screenshot button, and show it again after unlocking
      Print              should open no screenshot UI
      Shift+Print        should save no new file into ~/Pictures/Screenshots
